INSERT INTO pages (slug, title, description, body_html, status, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('dog-food-brand-reviews','Honest Dog Food Brand Reviews 2026','Honest dog food brand reviews from the Pet Pros — what''s really in each brand, how it''s made, our top picks, and the drawbacks most reviews skip.','<p class="deck">Honest, vet-informed reviews of the dog food brands you''ll actually find in US stores — what''s really in the bag, how it''s made, which products are worth buying, and the drawbacks most reviews quietly leave out.</p>

<p>There are two kinds of dog food review online. The first is a list of affiliate links dressed up as an opinion. The second is a hit piece written by someone who has never fed the food. Neither helps you stand in an aisle and decide what to put in your dog''s bowl.</p>
<p>Our reviews are written from a nutrition-first perspective: we look at what the food is made of, how it''s processed, what the manufacturer''s safety record and certifications actually are, and who each product genuinely suits. Then we tell you the downsides — because a review with no criticism isn''t a review, it''s an advert.</p>

<h2>🔍 How We Review a Dog Food Brand</h2>
<p>Every brand on this page is assessed against the same six things, in this order:</p>
<ol class="method-list">
  <li><b>Ingredients and meat content.</b> Named meat sources rather than vague "meat meal", how far down the list the first non-meat ingredient appears, and whether organ and bone are included — because muscle meat alone isn''t complete nutrition for a dog.</li>
  <li><b>How it''s processed.</b> High-heat extrusion, freeze-drying, air-drying, gentle cooking or raw all affect how much of the original nutrition survives — and how the food needs to be stored and handled.</li>
  <li><b>Safety record and certifications.</b> Manufacturing standards, third-party certifications, batch testing, pathogen controls, and recall history. This is the section most reviews skip entirely, and it''s arguably the most important one.</li>
  <li><b>Nutritional adequacy.</b> Whether recipes carry an AAFCO complete-and-balanced statement, and for which life stage. "Complete and balanced for adult maintenance" is not the same as suitable for a growing puppy.</li>
  <li><b>Real-world value.</b> Cost per day for a real dog, not cost per bag. A premium topper over a good kibble often beats an expensive food fed alone.</li>
  <li><b>Who it isn''t for.</b> Every food has dogs it doesn''t suit — health conditions, life stages, allergies, or households where the handling requirements aren''t practical. We name them.</li>
</ol>

<div class="vet-tip">
  <h4>💚 Where we stand on affiliate links</h4>
  <p>Some links on our reviews are Amazon affiliate links, and we may earn a small commission at no extra cost to you. It doesn''t change what we recommend — you''ll find plenty of "buy the cheaper one" and "we wouldn''t feed this" in our reviews, and we routinely point people at a $20 trial size rather than a $90 bag. Full details on our <a href="/affiliate-disclosure">affiliate disclosure</a> page.</p>
</div>

<h2>⭐ Brands We''ve Reviewed</h2>

<div class="review-card">
  <div class="rc-score"><span class="n">Raw</span><span class="l">Specialist</span></div>
  <div class="rc-body">
    <div class="rc-cat">Freeze-dried raw · Kibble · Wet</div>
    <h3><a href="/blog/stella-and-chewys-dog-food-review">Stella &amp; Chewy''s Review</a></h3>
    <p>Founded in 2003 after a rescue dog the shelter''s vet advised against adopting recovered on a raw diet. The category leader in freeze-dried raw, and one of the first to adopt high-pressure processing for pathogen control. We cover the founding story, the confusing product lines, our top 10 picks across all three formats, the honest drawbacks — and five homemade treat recipes.</p>
    <div class="rc-tags"><span>95% meat, organ &amp; bone</span><span>HPP since 2007</span><span>Made in Wisconsin</span><span>Premium price</span></div>
    <a class="rc-btn" href="/blog/stella-and-chewys-dog-food-review">Read the full review →</a>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="review-card soon">
  <div class="rc-score"><span class="n">Soon</span><span class="l">In progress</span></div>
  <div class="rc-body">
    <div class="rc-cat">Coming next</div>
    <h3>More brands on the way</h3>
    <p>We''re working through the brands people ask about most — including the big supermarket names, the vet-recommended prescription ranges, and the boutique fresh-food subscriptions. Each gets the same six-point assessment.</p>
    <p style="margin-bottom:0"><strong>Want a specific brand reviewed?</strong> <a href="/contact">Tell us which one</a> and we''ll add it to the list.</p>
  </div>
</div>

<h2>🏷️ What We Check on Every Label</h2>
<p>You can apply most of this yourself in the aisle, whatever brand you''re holding.</p>

<h3>The first five ingredients tell you most of the story</h3>
<p>Ingredients are listed by weight before cooking, so look for <strong>named</strong> meats — "chicken", "lamb", "salmon" — rather than "meat meal", "animal derivatives" or "poultry by-product". A named meat meal (like "chicken meal") isn''t a bad thing, incidentally: it''s meat with the water already removed, so it''s actually more concentrated protein than fresh chicken listed at the same position.</p>

<h3>Watch for ingredient splitting</h3>
<p>If you see peas, pea protein, pea fibre and pea starch listed separately, that''s one ingredient split into four so each lands lower on the list. Add them together mentally and the picture often changes considerably.</p>

<h3>Find the AAFCO statement</h3>
<p>Somewhere on the bag, usually in small print near the feeding guide, is a nutritional adequacy statement. It will say the food is complete and balanced for <strong>growth</strong>, <strong>adult maintenance</strong>, or <strong>all life stages</strong> — or that it''s intended for <strong>intermittent or supplemental feeding only</strong>, which means it is a topper, not a meal. That last one catches people out regularly.</p>

<h3>Read the guaranteed analysis in context</h3>
<p>Protein and fat percentages on wet and dry food can''t be compared directly, because wet food is mostly water. To compare fairly you need dry matter basis — but as a rough guide, wet food percentages need roughly quadrupling before you hold them next to a kibble figure.</p>

<h3>Check the life stage matches your dog</h3>
<p>Puppies, and especially large-breed puppies, have genuinely different calcium and phosphorus requirements — too much during growth affects joint development. Seniors generally need fewer calories but not less protein. "All life stages" food is formulated to the highest requirement in the range, which means it can be richer than an older, less active dog needs.</p>

<div class="vet-warning">
  <h4>⚠️ When to ignore a review — including ours</h4>
  <p>If your dog has a diagnosed condition — pancreatitis, kidney disease, liver disease, diabetes, food allergies, or a heart condition — the right food is the one your vet recommends, not the one that scores best in a general review. Prescription diets exist because they''re formulated to therapeutic specifications that ordinary retail food isn''t. Always discuss a diet change with your vet if your dog is being treated for anything.</p>
</div>

<h2>🍪 Free Printable</h2>
<p>Our homemade dog treat recipes — pupsicles, training cookies, no-bake bites, enrichment Kongs and birthday pupcakes — plus the ingredients that are toxic to dogs. Free to download and share, no sign-up needed.</p>
<p><a class="cta-btn cta-green" href="/free-guides">Browse all free guides →</a></p>

<h2>The Bottom Line</h2>
<p>The best dog food is the one that suits <strong>your</strong> dog''s age, health and activity level, that you can afford to feed consistently, and that your dog will actually eat. A perfect food refused at the bowl is worth nothing.</p>
<p>Use these reviews to narrow the field, check the label yourself using the points above, transition slowly over ten days, and judge the result by what you see over the following two months — coat condition, energy, weight, and stool quality. Your dog gives you better feedback than any review can.</p>
